Z||000000||ur2biteme||07-19-2000||09:01 AM||||i have a line right now that i'm contemplating changing my current clutch line (held together with 3 compression fittings, scary) with.  it was specially made with fittings from the master and slave of another fiero, so it's not only metric, but i know it'll fit.<P>The hose was made by a rubber company and has a similar inside diamater to the original steel line, but it is flexible, and has TWO sections of braiding within the rubber, a textile braid and a steel braid.  it is rated at 3000p.s.i. continuous, and a burst pressure at 12000p.s.i.  it is an industrial hose rated for chemical use.  I was told that it would burst before it would absorb pressure.<P>i want to know what the approximate pressure the hydraulic system puts out to the clutch release arm.  i'm thinking that the line is WAY more than necessacary.<P>also, because the line is flexible, and my experience in bleeding the 13 frickin feet of hydraulic line on this thing, i was wondering if you guys had an easier idea on bleeding this thing, since the whole line is flexible.  <P>i thought about hooking it to the master, having someone pump the pedal on command, hold the hose, without routing it yet, as high as i can from the master, and bleed it by having them push the pedal, put my thumb over the hole, have them release and repeat until fluid comes out w/o air, capping the line, then all i'd have to do is bleed the slave.  any thoughts on this would be helpful.  thanks.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>UR2BITEME||||reg||5
Z||000001||Taijiguy||07-19-2000||09:53 AM||||I can't speak to the quality of the line, or the usefulness of replacing the steel line with flexible, but if you're planning on doing it, I can tell you this:<P>The pressure the line will experience is directly related to the release pressure of your clutch. If I accurately recall my fluid dynamics, it would work something like this:<P>(using nice round numbers, and not complicating it with intensifying components, etc.)<P>If the slave cyinder has a surface area of 2 square inches, and the master cylinder has a surface area of 1 square inch, then the slave piston will move half as far as the master cylinder piston. (Since the piston in the master is half the size of the piston in the slave-half the displacement) (This is called the mechanical advantage) So, if the slave clynder experiences an outside force on its piston of let's say 300 psi, since the slave has a piston size of two square inches, it would experience the outside force divided by its surface area; 300/2=150, This is the pressure that would be felt throughout the entire system. So since the master cylinder piston has a surface area of 1 square inch, it would experience system pressure times one; 150x1=150. Not accounting for any leverage at the pedal, you would experience 150 psi at the pedal. SO basically, you need to know the pressure required to release the clutch at the relaease arm, the surface area of the slave cylinder piston, and then calculate what the pressure is from that information. That will give you the system operating pressure. <BR>All technical information aside, I'm SURE a burst pressure of 12,000 psi is PLENTY substantial.<BR>As for bleeding the system; here's the best method I know of for one man operation:<P>Remove the bleed valve and wrap the threads with teflon tape.<BR>Replace the bleed valve, leave it open.<BR>Place a length of hose on the bleed valve, and extend the opposite end into a container of system fluid.<BR>FIll the master reservoir, and start pumping.<BR>Maintain master reservoir level (it only takes a few pumps to drain it!)<BR>Pump until no more bubbles appear (approx. 1 million strokes)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Tighten bleed valve BEFORE removing the hose.<P>Sorry about the length of the post, but I hope it provides you with some useful information.<P>-Taiji<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 07-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ur2biteme||07-20-2000||06:01 AM||||thanks for the info.  i got the line because i kept having problems with the section of braided line under the master cylinder leaking.  i replaced the entire line twice, and wanted to get something i wouldn't have to screw with much.  the master cyl is new, and the slave isn't, but i know is good....<P>By the way.  Steel clutch pedals bend too. just found out that mine was not getting proper throw either.....disengaged close to the floor, took the pedal out, sure enough.......<P>i've also had luck using both methods at once, gravity bleeding and the traditional method.  when i get frustrated, i take a can of 1/4 full brake fluid (quart size) turn it upside down in the resivoir, pull the bleeder valve and walk away for about a half hour.......||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||07-20-2000||08:05 AM||||I've wondered if that method would work, it seems it wouldn't cause the bubbles to actually move since it is such a slow movement of fluid.<BR>I did just read another thread where someone indicated that a flywheel in need of turning would cause the clutch to not disengage. I never thought of that, but it makes sense.....just a thought.<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
